[
  {
    "title": "The customer journey",
    "old": "Digital marketing uses online channels to reach people, earn trust and encourage a useful action. A customer may discover a business, compare alternatives, buy and later return. These stages are awareness, consideration, conversion and retention. Match each message to its stage. A bakery could use a short video for awareness, a menu page for consideration and WhatsApp for orders. Owned channels include your website and email list; paid channels include advertising; earned reach includes genuine recommendations.\n\nPractice: Choose a business and write one suitable message and channel for each of the four stages. Decide which customer action matters most.",
    "body": "Map a customer journey\n\nImagine FreshRise Bakery, a fictional neighbourhood bakery. Your job is to move someone who has never heard of it towards a first order and then a repeat purchase. The same person may move backwards or skip a stage, so choose a useful next action rather than assuming every follower is ready to buy.\n\nStep 1: Identify discovery\nWrite down where a new customer might first notice the bakery. Make a short educational post showing how to choose fresh bread. Use a simple photo and a location caption; measure reach rather than orders at this stage.\n[[visual|Awareness|Channel|Instagram post|Measure|Reach]]\n\nStep 2: Build consideration\nCreate a simple menu page with prices, collection hours and real product photos. Link from the post to the menu. Ask a friend to locate the price on a phone without help.\n[[visual|Consideration|Page|Menu and prices|Measure|Menu visits]]\n\nStep 3: Design conversion and retention\nAdd one clear order action and test it from a phone. After delivery, ask for feedback and offer an opt-in reminder for future orders. Track confirmed orders separately from enquiries.\n[[visual|Conversion → retention|Action|Order / opt-in reminder|Measure|Orders / repeat orders]]\n\nTry it yourself\nDraw four journey stages for your own fictional business. Match each to a channel, message and metric.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|A customer compares menu prices. Which stage?|Consideration: the customer is evaluating the offer.]]\n[[check|Which metric fits awareness?|Reach, not confirmed orders.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://academy.hubspot.com/courses/digital-marketing]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Audience, goals and offers",
    "old": "Begin with a customer need, not a platform. Define an audience using relevant needs, location and buying behaviour. For a cleaning service, busy households needing weekend appointments are more useful than everyone. A SMART goal is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound: for example, 20 qualified enquiries in 30 days. Define a qualified enquiry as someone in your service area requesting a service you offer. A value proposition explains who you help, the outcome and why you are credible. A call to action (CTA) tells people what to do next.\n\nPractice: Write a customer persona, a SMART goal, an offer and one CTA.",
    "body": "Write an audience brief\n\nAn audience is a group with a shared need, not a list of stereotypes. A fictional cleaning service needs weekend bookings. Work backwards from the booking to the customer problem. Use interviews or anonymised enquiry patterns where authorised; distinguish evidence from assumptions.\n\nStep 1: Create a persona\nOpen a blank document. Write the customer need, location, barrier and preferred action. Avoid unnecessary personal details. Mark unverified assumptions so you can test them later.\n[[visual|Persona worksheet|Need|Weekend home cleaning|Barrier|Limited weekday time]]\n\nStep 2: Set a SMART goal\nChoose a result, number and deadline. Define qualified enquiries and where you will count them. If no baseline exists, treat the target as a hypothesis.\n[[visual|Goal worksheet|Target|20 qualified enquiries|Deadline|30 days]]\n\nStep 3: Write an offer and CTA\nExplain the customer benefit and service area in one sentence. Add a single next step, for example request a quote. Check the promise is accurate and can be fulfilled.\n[[visual|Offer card|Benefit|Weekend cleaning slots|CTA|Request a quote]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCreate a one-page brief with persona, goal, offer, CTA and measurement source.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Is 500 likes a qualified lead goal?|No. Count enquiries that meet your qualification criteria.]]\n[[check|What does CTA mean?|Call to action: the next action you want a reader to take.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://academy.hubspot.com/courses/digital-marketing]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Content strategy and calendars",
    "old": "Content should answer a customer question or make a decision easier. Use three pillars: education, genuine proof and offers. A caption can begin with a relevant hook, explain one benefit and end with one CTA. Reuse ideas across formats instead of copying other creators. An explanation can become a carousel, short video and email. In Google Sheets, create columns for date, platform, topic, format, CTA, owner and status. Include time for responding to customers.\n\nPractice: Plan seven posts for one business, including two educational posts, genuine proof where available and one clear offer. Write a measurable purpose for each.",
    "body": "Create a content calendar\n\nA calendar turns ideas into a publishing routine. Use a blank spreadsheet in Google Sheets or any free spreadsheet app. Choose one fictional local business, define one objective and keep the first week manageable.\n\nStep 1: Set up the sheet\nIn a new spreadsheet, label the columns Date, Channel, Topic, Format, CTA, Owner, Status and KPI. Freeze the header if available. Each row represents one content item.\n[[visual|Calendar sheet|Columns|Date / topic / CTA|Status|Draft]]\n\nStep 2: Fill seven rows\nAssign useful topics: Monday FAQ, Tuesday genuine product demonstration, Wednesday tip, Thursday behind the scenes, Friday offer, Saturday reminder and Sunday review. Adapt the format to the audience.\n[[visual|Example row|Topic|How to choose fresh bread|Format|Carousel]]\n\nStep 3: Review and measure\nDraft the caption with a hook, benefit and one CTA. Check rights to images. Set a scheduled time or reminder, assign the person responsible and enter results after publishing.\n[[visual|Publishing workflow|Next action|Review → schedule|KPI|Qualified messages]]\n\nTry it yourself\nMake a seven-row calendar with one CTA and one KPI per row. No real publishing required.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Why add a KPI column?|To connect each post to a measurable purpose.]]\n[[check|Can you invent a testimonial for a proof post?|No. Use only authentic, permitted evidence.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://academy.hubspot.com/courses/content-marketing]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Canva and CapCut",
    "old": "Canva helps create graphics, carousels and simple brand assets. Select a format, replace sample text, use consistent colours, one heading and enough whitespace. Export the result and check it on your phone. CapCut helps edit short videos: import authorised footage, trim pauses, lead with a useful hook and add readable captions. For vertical social video, start with a 9:16 canvas and keep important text clear of interface overlays. Use licensed music and images. Save an editable copy and a final export.\n\nPractice: Draft one Canva graphic and a 15-second video storyboard with a hook, benefit and CTA. A written storyboard is sufficient.",
    "body": "Design a graphic and a short video\n\nCanva and CapCut are two different creative tools. Use a fictional bakery promotion to learn layout and editing. The illustrations are simulated teaching examples, not current product screenshots. Use your own footage or licensed media; check plan limits.\n\nStep 1: Create a Canva graphic\nOpen Canva, choose Create a design and select a suitable social-post format. Start blank or select a free template. Replace the sample headline, image and CTA with your own. Keep the text readable at phone size.\n[[visual|Canva-style design|Headline|Fresh bread this Saturday|CTA|View menu]]\n\nStep 2: Export and inspect\nUse the sharing/download controls to export a suitable image format, such as PNG. Open the exported file on a phone and check contrast, spelling, cropping and the CTA. Save the editable project separately.\n[[visual|Design export|File type|PNG|Check|Readable at phone size]]\n\nStep 3: Edit a CapCut video\nStart a project and import a permitted clip. Arrange three short segments, trim pauses, use a vertical canvas where appropriate, add readable captions, and preview before exporting. A silent captioned video works for this practice.\n[[visual|Video timeline|0–5 s|Hook: fresh from the oven|5–15 s|Benefit → menu CTA]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCreate one graphic and a 15-second storyboard. Export only if you have the tools; a sketch is acceptable.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|What should you verify before exporting?|Readability, crop, spelling, content rights and the CTA.]]\n[[check|Does this practice require music or paid assets?|No. Use permitted free assets or your own content.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://www.canva.com/help/download-or-purchase/]]\n[[source|https://www.capcut.com/resource/how-to-trim-a-video-in-capcut-and-check-captions-and-audio]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Set up business profiles",
    "old": "Select platforms according to your audience and capacity. Instagram is visual, Facebook can support local communities, LinkedIn suits many professional services and TikTok can support short discovery content. Use a recognisable profile image, consistent name, accurate contact details and a bio explaining the offer. Test your link on a phone as a visitor. Enable two-factor authentication and use individual staff access instead of shared passwords. Meta Business Suite can help manage eligible Facebook and Instagram accounts, messages, scheduled posts and insights; interfaces may change.\n\nPractice: Audit a business profile and draft an improved bio, contact link and three content ideas.",
    "body": "Set up a professional profile\n\nA good business profile tells visitors what the business does and how to contact it. Choose a channel where your audience is active and only use an account you own or manage with permission.\n\nStep 1: Audit the profile\nLook at the profile as a visitor. Check name, icon, bio, contact link and location. List missing or inconsistent information rather than opening multiple accounts immediately.\n[[visual|Profile audit|Business|FreshRise Bakery|Missing|Opening hours]]\n\nStep 2: Prepare the bio\nWrite a benefit, location and action. Use a recognisable image, accurate hours and a working link. Enable two-factor authentication and grant individual access instead of sharing passwords.\n[[visual|Profile preview|Bio|Fresh bread / Local pickup|Link|View menu]]\n\nStep 3: Plan in Meta Business Suite\nFor an eligible linked Facebook or Instagram account, open Business Suite and select the appropriate page. Draft a post, preview it, and save it or choose a future schedule if authorised. Do not publish during this exercise.\n[[visual|Business Suite example|Destination|Fictional Facebook page|Action|Save as draft]]\n\nTry it yourself\nWrite an improved bio and draft one post. List two account security checks.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Why test the contact link on a phone?|To verify visitors can reach the intended destination.]]\n[[check|Should staff share the owner password?|No. Use individual permissions and two-factor authentication.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://eventsmanager.facebook.com/business/learn/lessons/introduction-meta-business-suite]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Organic reach and customer care",
    "old": "Organic marketing distributes content without paying for that placement. Useful content, consistency and genuine interaction support discovery. Reach generally counts accounts exposed; impressions count displays, including repeats. Engagement includes actions such as comments, saves and shares. Answer enquiries respectfully and move sensitive information into an approved private channel. WhatsApp Business can organise conversations with labels and quick replies. Do not send unsolicited promotions or share customer photos without permission. Track genuine enquiries rather than purchased followers.\n\nPractice: Write three responses for a pricing enquiry, delivery question and complaint. Use fictional details only.",
    "body": "Handle enquiries and organic content\n\nOrganic distribution means no paid placement for that post. Consistent helpful content and genuine responses can encourage trust, but reach is not guaranteed. Keep customer care separate from public disclosure of private information.\n\nStep 1: Plan an organic post\nChoose a common question from an authorised source. Write a short answer with one helpful example. End with a question or relevant CTA, not an unrelated hashtag list.\n[[visual|Post draft|Topic|How to book pickup|CTA|Check available times]]\n\nStep 2: Organise conversations\nIn an authorised WhatsApp Business account, use conversation labels where available, such as New enquiry and Awaiting reply. Draft quick responses for price, delivery and complaints. Never ask for card data in chat.\n[[visual|Inbox example|Label|Awaiting reply|Next action|Send approved quote]]\n\nStep 3: Read basic insights\nRecord reach, impressions, comments, saves, enquiries and confirmed sales in a sheet. Compare similar content over the same date range. If reach is high but enquiries low, test the offer or contact link.\n[[visual|Insights example|Reach|500 accounts|Enquiries|12 qualified]]\n\nTry it yourself\nDraft three customer responses and a one-week insights log.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Can impressions exceed reach?|Yes, an account can see a post more than once.]]\n[[check|Where should sensitive complaint details go?|An approved private and secure process, not public comments.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://business.facebook.com/business/learn/courses]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Search engine optimisation",
    "old": "SEO helps search engines discover and understand useful pages. Search intent means what a person hopes to achieve. Someone searching how to register a business needs guidance; someone searching registration service near me may want a quote. Use a descriptive page title, clear headings, helpful original content, internal links and appropriate image alternative text. Make the page fast and mobile-friendly. A sitemap can assist discovery but cannot guarantee indexing or ranking. Google Search Console helps monitor search visibility and diagnose indexing issues. Avoid buying links or stuffing keywords.\n\nPractice: Write a page title, heading and outline for a local service. Explain its intended search query.",
    "body": "Improve a page for search\n\nSearch engines need to discover pages and understand useful content. SEO is not a guaranteed ranking service. Work on one authorised website or a mock page, and match the content to a realistic search intent.\n\nStep 1: Choose a query\nThink of the customer's question. Compare informational intent such as how to clean a sofa with commercial intent such as sofa cleaning service. Pick one page goal and write a title and main heading.\n[[visual|Page outline|Search intent|Sofa cleaning service|Page title|Sofa cleaning in Ado-Ekiti]]\n\nStep 2: Improve the page\nWrite a clear introduction, answer common questions, add accurate service details, descriptive internal links and appropriate image alt text. Compress images and test the mobile layout. Avoid repeated keywords and copied articles.\n[[visual|Page checklist|Heading|Sofa cleaning service|Image alt|Technician cleaning a sofa]]\n\nStep 3: Inspect the URL\nIn a verified Search Console property, enter the full page URL in the inspection field. Review the index status and any reported issue. Use the live test when appropriate; a successful test does not guarantee inclusion in Search.\n[[visual|Search Console example|URL|example.com/sofa-cleaning|Result|Inspect indexing status]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCreate a page outline and an SEO checklist for one service. If you have no verified site, use a paper simulation.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Does requesting indexing guarantee a ranking?|No. Discovery, indexing and ranking are different.]]\n[[check|Why use descriptive headings?|They help readers and search systems understand the page.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289/]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Landing pages and local discovery",
    "old": "A landing page should match the promise in the message that brought visitors there. Present a clear benefit, relevant offer, genuine proof and one main CTA. Keep forms short and explain what happens next. Test the page, contact links and confirmation on a narrow phone screen. Eligible businesses can use Google Business Profile to manage their information on Search and Maps. Keep opening hours, service areas, photos and contact information accurate. Ask for genuine reviews without incentives or pressure.\n\nPractice: Sketch six blocks for a mobile landing page: headline, offer, benefits, proof, CTA and privacy or contact information.",
    "body": "Build a landing page and local profile\n\nA landing page is a focused destination for one offer. Local discovery uses accurate business information and useful content. Use a mock cleaning company and one enquiry objective.\n\nStep 1: Sketch the mobile page\nDraw a vertical layout: headline, offer, benefits, real proof, CTA and contact/privacy information. Put the main action near the top and repeat it only where useful. Avoid fabricated reviews.\n[[visual|Landing page wireframe|Headline|Weekend home cleaning|Primary CTA|Request a quote]]\n\nStep 2: Test the journey\nOpen a mock or authorised page on a phone. Check that the CTA leads to the correct form, required fields are understandable and a confirmation appears after a legitimate test. Do not request unnecessary information.\n[[visual|Form test|Fields|Name / contact / service|Success|Enquiry confirmation]]\n\nStep 3: Review Business Profile\nFor a verified eligible business, use Google Business Profile editing controls to update categories, hours, website and service area accurately. Review published changes and request genuine feedback without incentives.\n[[visual|Local profile example|Hours|Sat 09:00–16:00|Service area|Actual served locations]]\n\nTry it yourself\nSketch a six-block page and audit a mock local listing.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|What should a landing page match?|The promise made in the ad or post.]]\n[[check|Can you create a fake office address for visibility?|No. Business information must be accurate and eligible.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://support.google.com/business/answer/3039617?hl=en]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Permission-based email marketing",
    "old": "Build an audience who expects your messages through a clear signup. Explain the purpose, follow applicable privacy and marketing requirements and provide a way to unsubscribe. Honour opt-outs; do not buy or scrape lists. An email needs an accurate sender, honest subject, useful message and CTA. Segment by relevant interests or purchase stage. A welcome email can introduce the business; a follow-up may answer a common question. Mailchimp and HubSpot offer campaign tools, with limits that vary by plan. Test emails on mobile and use authorised sending domains.\n\nPractice: Draft a welcome email and a helpful follow-up for a fictional opt-in subscriber.",
    "body": "Build an opt-in email campaign\n\nEmail marketing works when recipients expect relevant messages. Use fictional subscriber data and draft mode. Consent requirements vary; honour opt-outs and avoid scraped lists.\n\nStep 1: Prepare your audience\nCreate a signup purpose and record permission. In a Mailchimp-style workflow, select an audience of opted-in contacts and use tags or segments for relevant interests. Never upload real contacts without authority.\n[[visual|Audience example|Segment|Weekend offers opt-in|Permission|Subscribed]]\n\nStep 2: Draft the email\nCreate a regular email campaign in the tool. Choose recipients, sender name and honest subject. Write a short introduction, benefit and one CTA. Include the required unsubscribe method and sender information.\n[[visual|Email preview|Subject|Your weekend menu|CTA|View menu]]\n\nStep 3: Test before sending\nPreview desktop and mobile, send a test only to your authorised test address if appropriate, inspect links and confirm the audience and suppression list. Save as a draft for this class.\n[[visual|Pre-send checklist|Recipients|Test address only|Status|Draft — not sent]]\n\nTry it yourself\nWrite a welcome email and follow-up with a clear CTA and opt-out method.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|What happens after an opt-out?|Stop promotional contact and honour the unsubscribe.]]\n[[check|Why use a segment?|To send relevant messages to an appropriate subset.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://mailchimp.com/help/create-and-send-regular-email/]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "CRM and lead follow-up",
    "old": "A customer relationship management system (CRM) helps organise authorised customer records and follow-up tasks. A small business can start with a protected sheet containing enquiry date, requested service, consent status, stage and next action. Stages could be new, qualified, quoted, won and lost. Follow up when useful; stop promotional contact when requested. Restrict access and remove information you no longer need. Measure response time, qualified leads and completed sales. Email opens are imperfect signals; clicks and completed actions are often more useful.\n\nPractice: Create five fictional enquiries and assign each a stage and responsible next action.",
    "body": "Create a simple lead pipeline\n\nA CRM tracks the state of enquiries and the next responsible action. A protected spreadsheet is sufficient for beginners. Use invented names or anonymous references throughout this lesson.\n\nStep 1: Create a lead sheet\nAdd columns for reference, date, service, permission, stage, responsible person and next action. Use limited access and avoid unnecessary customer data.\n[[visual|CRM worksheet|Reference|LEAD-001|Stage|New]]\n\nStep 2: Move an enquiry\nQualify a fictional request by checking whether it matches the service and area. Move it from New to Qualified, then Quoted only when a quote is sent. Mark Won only for a confirmed sale.\n[[visual|Pipeline example|Current stage|Qualified|Next stage|Quoted]]\n\nStep 3: Set follow-up and measure\nAssign a responsible owner and next date. If the customer declines or opts out, record the appropriate status and stop unwanted contact. Calculate how many qualified leads became sales.\n[[visual|Follow-up record|Owner|Sales team|Next action|Review quote tomorrow]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCreate five fictional records and assign a stage, owner and next action to each.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Is every new enquiry a sale?|No. Track new, qualified, quoted and won separately.]]\n[[check|What happens if someone opts out?|Stop promotional follow-up and keep only necessary records.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://academy.hubspot.com/courses/email-marketing-certification-en]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Meta Ads and campaign design",
    "old": "Paid advertising buys distribution. Start with a business objective and select a corresponding campaign objective in Ads Manager. A campaign defines the goal, an ad set typically controls delivery choices and an ad contains creative and destination. Exact settings vary. Set a spend ceiling, suitable audience, clear offer and tracking plan. Compare two creative variations by changing one main variable at a time. Check the landing page before launching. A boosted post can be simpler; Ads Manager offers more controls. Neither guarantees results.\n\nPractice: Plan a mock seven-day enquiry campaign with an audience, budget ceiling, two creative ideas and stop rule. Do not activate an ad.",
    "body": "Plan a Meta Ads campaign\n\nPaid advertising requires a clear objective, authorised budget and tracking. This tutorial is a mock exercise: do not enter payment details or launch ads. Ads Manager screens may differ by account and region.\n\nStep 1: Choose a campaign objective\nIn an authorised Ads Manager account, locate Campaigns and Create. For a service enquiry exercise, choose a suitable leads objective if available. Write the intended conversion and name the campaign clearly.\n[[visual|Campaign example|Objective|Leads|Name|Cleaning — enquiries]]\n\nStep 2: Configure the ad set\nSketch an ad set with a service area, delivery schedule and a budget ceiling. Use a fictional 7-day test with a maximum authorised total; verify actual budget behaviour before any live campaign. Identify where leads will arrive.\n[[visual|Ad set example|Schedule|7 days (simulation)|Ceiling|₦14,000 total]]\n\nStep 3: Create and review an ad\nPrepare one creative, accurate offer, destination and CTA. Duplicate the concept on paper to test one change, such as headline. Review destination, tracking and all spend controls; keep it unpublished.\n[[visual|Ad preview|Headline|Book weekend cleaning|CTA|Request quote]]\n\nTry it yourself\nPrepare a mock campaign brief and stop rule. Do not spend money.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Which level holds the main objective?|Campaign level; the ad set configures delivery and the ad contains creative.]]\n[[check|Should you launch this training campaign?|No. Keep it as a simulation or draft.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://en-gb.facebook.com/business/m/ads-manager-guide/creating-ads]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Google Search Ads",
    "old": "Google Search ads can reach people actively searching for a service. Group related keywords, write relevant ad text and link to a matching landing page. Keyword match types help determine eligible searches; negative keywords help exclude irrelevant searches. Set a budget and bidding approach aligned with your goal. Review search terms and meaningful conversions instead of assuming every click is valuable. A shoe-repair business may exclude searches for free repair tutorials if they do not match its service.\n\nPractice: Draft five keywords, three negative keywords, an ad headline and landing-page CTA for a fictional service. No spending is required.",
    "body": "Plan a Google Search campaign\n\nSearch ads connect an offer to relevant queries. This is a mock planning exercise. Use a fictional shoe repair shop, no billing or publishing. Keyword matching and automatic delivery settings can vary.\n\nStep 1: Group your keywords\nWrite search phrases relevant to shoe repair, such as shoe repair near me and boot sole replacement. Organise by service theme. Avoid broad irrelevant terms without a clear review plan.\n[[visual|Keyword worksheet|Ad group|Sole replacement|Keyword|boot sole replacement]]\n\nStep 2: Create negative keywords\nList queries that do not match the service, such as free DIY tutorials or jobs if the shop does not hire. Consider match type before excluding a word so you do not remove valuable searches.\n[[visual|Exclusion worksheet|Example|free DIY tutorial|Reason|Not a repair enquiry]]\n\nStep 3: Draft and review the ad\nUse a headline matching the keyword theme, honest description, relevant destination and clear CTA. Sketch a budget and conversion plan. In an authorised account, create a Search campaign draft and review search terms only if data exists.\n[[visual|Search ad example|Headline|Local boot sole repair|Destination|Repair quote page]]\n\nTry it yourself\nWrite five keywords, three exclusions and an ad with a matching CTA. No spending.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|What do negative keywords do?|Exclude eligible irrelevant searches according to the match type.]]\n[[check|Is every paid click a valuable lead?|No. Measure meaningful conversions and cost.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9510373]]\n[[source|https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2453972/]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "Marketing metrics",
    "old": "CTR is clicks divided by impressions times 100. CPC is ad spend divided by clicks. Conversion rate is conversions divided by the selected denominator, such as visits, times 100. CPA is spend divided by acquisitions. ROAS is attributed ad revenue divided by ad spend; it is not profit. Example: ₦10,000 spend, 5,000 impressions, 100 clicks and five purchases worth ₦30,000 gives CTR 2%, CPC ₦100, CPA ₦2,000 and ROAS 3x. Other business costs still matter.\n\nPractice: Calculate CTR, CPC and CPA for ₦6,000 spend, 3,000 impressions, 60 clicks and three leads. Answers: 2%, ₦100 and ₦2,000.",
    "body": "Calculate core marketing metrics\n\nMetrics help diagnose performance, not prove causation. Use a spreadsheet with a fictional ad report. Define the measurement period, currency and conversion denominator before comparing campaigns.\n\nStep 1: Enter the raw numbers\nCreate columns for spend, impressions, clicks, purchases and attributed revenue. Enter ₦10,000, 5,000, 100, 5 and ₦30,000 in one row. Keep raw values separate from calculated metrics.\n[[visual|Raw report|Spend|₦10,000|Clicks|100]]\n\nStep 2: Calculate rates and costs\nCTR = clicks / impressions × 100 = 2%. CPC = spend / clicks = ₦100. Purchase rate using clicks = 5 / 100 × 100 = 5%. CPA = spend / purchases = ₦2,000. Leave undefined ratios blank when the denominator is zero.\n[[visual|Metrics worksheet|CTR|2%|CPA|₦2,000]]\n\nStep 3: Interpret the results\nROAS = attributed revenue / spend = 3x. It excludes other costs, returns and attribution uncertainty. Compare the CPA with a business-approved maximum and recommend a specific next test.\n[[visual|Decision example|ROAS|3× revenue/spend|Next test|Improve landing page]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCalculate CTR, CPC and CPA from ₦6,000 spend, 3,000 impressions, 60 clicks and 3 leads. Then explain one decision.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|What is CTR for 60 clicks from 3,000 impressions?|2%.]]\n[[check|Does ROAS of 3x mean profit is 3x?|No. It compares attributed revenue to advertising spend.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/10917952]]"
  },
  {
    "title": "GA4, campaign tags and reporting",
    "old": "Google Analytics 4 measures website and app interactions as events. A key event represents an important action such as a completed enquiry form. Verify tracking and honour applicable consent requirements. UTM tags identify campaign traffic: utm_source is where it came from, utm_medium is channel type and utm_campaign names the promotion. Use consistent names, for example https://example.com/?utm_source=instagram&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=november_offer. Never include personal information in URLs. Search Console measures search visibility; GA4 measures site behaviour. Reports can differ.\n\nPractice: Write a sample tagged link and a short report describing results, a possible explanation and a next test.",
    "body": "Read GA4 and tag campaign links\n\nGA4 records events and can help understand site behaviour. Use the official Analytics demo account to explore without changing real tracking. Do not include personal data in tracking URLs.\n\nStep 1: Build a tagged link\nStart with a fictional URL such as https://example.com/menu. Add utm_source=instagram, utm_medium=social and utm_campaign=weekend_menu using ? for the first parameter and & between parameters. Keep naming consistent.\n[[visual|UTM worksheet|Source / medium|instagram / social|Campaign|weekend_menu]]\n\nStep 2: Explore the demo report\nOpen the official GA4 demo account if available to your Google account. In Reports, find Acquisition and Traffic acquisition (navigation may vary). Compare session source/medium and sessions for one date range.\n[[visual|GA4-style report|Session source|google / organic|Measure|Sessions / key events]]\n\nStep 3: Write a useful report\nSelect a meaningful key event such as completed enquiry. Compare results with the stated goal and note tracking or consent limitations. Recommend one change, for example improve the offer page, rather than claiming causation.\n[[visual|Weekly report|Finding|Social sessions increased|Next test|Improve enquiry CTA]]\n\nTry it yourself\nCreate a tagged example URL and a three-sentence report.\n\nQuick knowledge check\n[[check|Which parameter identifies the source?|utm_source.]]\n[[check|Why not include emails in UTM tags?|URLs can expose personal information in logs and analytics.]]\n\nFurther reading\n[[source|https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6367342?hl=en]]\n[[source|https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/12923437]]\n[[source|https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/11242870]]"
  },
